# CrossEngin domain pack: comparative political science (v1)
#
# ~65 atoms + ~60 implications. Descriptive comparative-political-
# science content at academic-reference level (Britannica, Oxford
# Handbook of Comparative Politics, Cambridge Handbook of Political
# Institutions). Answers the vision's "political analysis" call-out.
#
# EDITORIAL STANCE:
#   * Descriptive only. This pack states which political SYSTEMS exist,
#     what defining features they have as taught in comparative
#     government, which INSTITUTIONS make up common governmental
#     structures, and which DOCUMENTS have shaped modern political
#     order. It states what ideologies TEACH -- as documented -- not
#     whether those teachings are correct.
#   * Ontological is-a edges (democracy IS a political_system) get
#     FORMAL strength because they are categorizations, not truth
#     claims about which system is best.
#   * Ideology descriptions get EMPIRICAL strength -- they reflect a
#     reference source's summary, not a normative endorsement.
#   * Historical political events (French Revolution, fall of the
#     Berlin Wall) get high belief where scholarly consensus is
#     strong; where scholars disagree on interpretation, belief is
#     lower.
#   * No claim about any current government, party, or living figure.
#     The pack targets the STABLE content of comparative politics
#     that would appear in a decade-old undergraduate textbook and
#     still be accurate today.
#
# Ingest via:
#   /ingest cerec data/packs/politics.cerec politics \
#           src:pack:politics:v1

KG politics
SRC src:pack:politics:v1

# ============================================================================
# 1. Root ontology
# ============================================================================

ATOM political_system 3 950
ATOM political_ideology 3 950
ATOM political_institution 3 950
ATOM political_document 3 950
ATOM political_concept 3 950
ATOM international_body 3 950
ATOM person 3 950
ATOM historical_event 3 950

# ============================================================================
# 2. Political systems (structural taxonomy)
# ============================================================================

ATOM democracy 3 1000
ATOM republic 3 950
ATOM monarchy 3 950
ATOM authoritarianism 3 900
ATOM totalitarianism 3 900
ATOM oligarchy 3 900
ATOM theocracy 3 900
ATOM anarchism 3 850
ATOM federalism 3 950
ATOM unitary_state 3 950
ATOM confederation 3 900

IMP democracy political_system FORMAL
IMP republic political_system FORMAL
IMP monarchy political_system FORMAL
IMP authoritarianism political_system FORMAL
IMP totalitarianism political_system FORMAL
IMP oligarchy political_system FORMAL
IMP theocracy political_system FORMAL
IMP federalism political_system FORMAL
IMP unitary_state political_system FORMAL
IMP confederation political_system FORMAL

# Sub-types of democracy + monarchy (from Britannica's comparative-government entry)
ATOM representative_democracy 3 950
ATOM direct_democracy 3 900
ATOM constitutional_monarchy 3 950
ATOM absolute_monarchy 3 900
ATOM parliamentary_democracy 3 950
ATOM presidential_democracy 3 950

IMP representative_democracy democracy EMPIRICAL
IMP direct_democracy democracy EMPIRICAL
IMP parliamentary_democracy democracy EMPIRICAL
IMP presidential_democracy democracy EMPIRICAL
IMP constitutional_monarchy monarchy EMPIRICAL
IMP absolute_monarchy monarchy EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 3. Political ideologies (documented positions, not endorsements)
# ============================================================================

ATOM liberalism 3 950
ATOM conservatism 3 950
ATOM socialism 3 950
ATOM communism 3 950
ATOM fascism 3 900
ATOM libertarianism 3 900
ATOM social_democracy 3 900
ATOM nationalism 3 900
ATOM populism 3 900
ATOM green_politics 3 900

IMP liberalism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP conservatism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP socialism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP communism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP fascism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP libertarianism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP social_democracy political_ideology FORMAL
IMP nationalism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP populism political_ideology FORMAL
IMP green_politics political_ideology FORMAL

# ============================================================================
# 4. Political institutions (branches / offices / bodies)
# ============================================================================

ATOM legislature 3 950
ATOM executive_branch 3 950
ATOM judiciary 3 950
ATOM parliament 3 950
ATOM congress 3 950
ATOM senate 3 900
ATOM supreme_court 3 900
ATOM cabinet 3 900
ATOM prime_minister 3 900
ATOM president 3 900
ATOM monarch 3 900

IMP legislature political_institution FORMAL
IMP executive_branch political_institution FORMAL
IMP judiciary political_institution FORMAL
IMP parliament legislature EMPIRICAL
IMP congress legislature EMPIRICAL
IMP senate legislature EMPIRICAL
IMP supreme_court judiciary EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 5. Key documents that shaped modern political order
# ============================================================================

ATOM magna_carta 1 950
ATOM constitution 3 950
ATOM us_constitution 1 950
ATOM us_bill_of_rights 1 950
ATOM declaration_of_independence 1 950
ATOM universal_declaration_of_human_rights 1 950
ATOM us_independence_1776 1 950
ATOM french_declaration_of_rights_1789 1 900

IMP magna_carta political_document FORMAL
IMP us_constitution political_document FORMAL
IMP us_bill_of_rights political_document FORMAL
IMP declaration_of_independence political_document FORMAL
IMP universal_declaration_of_human_rights political_document FORMAL
IMP french_declaration_of_rights_1789 political_document FORMAL

# ============================================================================
# 6. Foundational political concepts (documented in comparative politics)
# ============================================================================

ATOM rule_of_law 3 950
ATOM separation_of_powers 3 950
ATOM checks_and_balances 3 950
ATOM universal_suffrage 3 950
ATOM civil_liberties 3 950
ATOM human_rights 3 950
ATOM sovereignty 3 950
ATOM citizenship 3 950
ATOM social_contract 3 900

IMP rule_of_law political_concept FORMAL
IMP separation_of_powers political_concept FORMAL
IMP checks_and_balances political_concept FORMAL
IMP universal_suffrage political_concept FORMAL
IMP civil_liberties political_concept FORMAL
IMP human_rights political_concept FORMAL
IMP sovereignty political_concept FORMAL
IMP citizenship political_concept FORMAL

# ============================================================================
# 7. International bodies (well-documented, treaty-established)
# ============================================================================

ATOM united_nations 1 1000
ATOM european_union 1 1000
ATOM world_trade_organization 1 950
ATOM international_court_of_justice 1 950
ATOM nato 1 950

IMP united_nations international_body FORMAL
IMP european_union international_body FORMAL
IMP world_trade_organization international_body FORMAL
IMP international_court_of_justice international_body FORMAL
IMP nato international_body FORMAL

# ============================================================================
# 8. Well-documented political milestones (SHARED with world_history)
# ============================================================================
# Deliberate label overlap with world_history so cross-KG scan verifies
# both packs agree on shared political events.

ATOM french_revolution_1789 1 950
ATOM russian_revolution_1917 1 950
ATOM fall_of_berlin_wall_1989 1 950
ATOM un_founded_1945 1 950
ATOM us_civil_rights_act_1964 1 900

IMP french_revolution_1789 historical_event FORMAL
IMP russian_revolution_1917 historical_event FORMAL
IMP fall_of_berlin_wall_1989 historical_event FORMAL
IMP un_founded_1945 historical_event FORMAL
IMP us_civil_rights_act_1964 historical_event FORMAL
IMP un_founded_1945 united_nations EMPIRICAL

# ============================================================================
# 9. Cross-source citations
# ============================================================================

CITE src:pack:politics:v1 src:paper:doi:britannica-comparative-government supports
CITE src:pack:politics:v1 src:paper:doi:oxford-handbook-comparative-politics supports
CITE src:pack:politics:v1 src:paper:doi:cambridge-handbook-political-institutions supports
CITE src:pack:politics:v1 src:pack:world_history:v1 extends
CITE src:pack:politics:v1 src:pack:religion:v1 extends

# ============================================================================
# 10. Reinforcing observations for the highest-consensus claims
# ============================================================================

OBS democracy +1 1000
OBS united_nations +1 1000
OBS us_constitution +1 1000
OBS fall_of_berlin_wall_1989 +1 1000
